 * `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py` (cleans an n8n export before it is published; no settings) replaces every address with a scheme (`://`, also with JSON-escaped slashes `:\/\/`), every `data:` link that has a media type, every `javascript:` or `vbscript:` link and every e-mail address that is not `example.test`, `example.com` or `example.invalid`. One kept exception: a web address (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`) to `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` stays, up to the end of its path; its query string and fragment (`?...`, `#...`) are dropped, because an API key can sit in a query. An address with any other scheme to `localhost` (`file://localhost/...`, `postgres://localhost/...`) is replaced whole. A tab, a carriage return or a line feed right after a `localhost` host (a browser ignores it) turns the address into a look-alike: the address, the break and the next run of non-blank characters are replaced whole. Any other blank right after a `localhost` host (a space, a vertical tab, a form feed, a no-break space or another Unicode space; a browser keeps it in the user name) does the same, but only when the next run of non-blank characters holds an at-sign before any `/`, `?` or `#` (`http://localhost @host`); `http://localhost:5678/x y` and `http://localhost y` (words after a space) are not touched. Webhook ids (the id field) are never kept, but the webhook path is kept as written, so a secret inside a path stays. **Not cleaned** (read the output by eye before you publish it): an address without a scheme (`//host/path`, `host:5432`), a bare host name, a path that is not a URL, the scheme written with percent signs (`http%3A%2F%2F`) or with backslashes (`http:\\host`); a secret inside the path of a kept `localhost` address (`/webhook/<token>`), also as a `%3F`-encoded query inside the path (only a real `?` or `#` ends the path); a data: link without a media type (`data:,text`, `data:;base64,...`); an e-mail address written with the small at-sign U+FE6B or with an ideographic full stop in the domain; the keys of a dictionary inside node parameters (only the values are cleaned); and, in a node name, an expression that refers to another node by an old name when two names became the same (the second gets ` (2)`). Over-cleaning by design: an address that an expression completes, such as `"http://localhost:5678/x?" + $json.q`, is read as a plain address and loses its `?` and what follows on the same run of characters. Likewise a bare local address, a space and then an e-mail address (`http://localhost jane@example.test`) loses the e-mail address too.
