 Everything here uses made-up data and loopback addresses only. No real model, mail or CRM service is ever called.
 Run every tool from the repository root with `PYTHONPATH=src:.` (on Windows use `;` between the entries: `src;.`), as in the other run commands in contract.md.
 
-Every file path you give a tool on its command line (`--log`, `--facts`, `--requests`, `--book`, `--catcher`, `--crm`, `--model-log`, `--drain`, `--json`, `--out`, `--control`) must lie inside the folder you started it in, the repository folder, or the system temporary folder; a path that leaves them (`..`, an absolute path elsewhere, a link that points out) is refused with a message that names the option, and nothing is read or written. To use another folder, name it in the environment variable `LEAD_DESK_PATH_ROOTS` (several folders separated by `:`, or `;` on Windows). A NEW output file may only use letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-` in its name (a file that already exists may have any name except one with a control character; a new name that ends with a dot or is a Windows device name such as `nul`, `aux`, `con`, `prn`, `com1` or `lpt1`, with or without an extension, is refused on every system, so the file also opens on Windows). A file that is read and does not exist is reported as "no such file". A name with accented letters is found also when it is written in the other Unicode form (macOS stores `é` as two characters, other systems as one), below the allowed folder (upper and lower case must match on macOS, and the allowed folder itself must be written the way the system reports it). That looser match is for files that are READ: a file that is WRITTEN (`--log`, `--facts`, `--json`, `--out`, `--control`, `out.json`) must be named exactly as the file system lists it, so a new name typed in one Unicode form never overwrites a file stored in another Unicode form (on Linux the two are different files); if only such a file exists the path is refused and the message says why. The folder you started the tool in counts as an allowed folder only when it is not the top of a file system (started in `/`) and not your home folder, because either would allow the whole disk or all of your files; start the tool in a project folder, or name the folder in `LEAD_DESK_PATH_ROOTS` (a folder named there is taken as written). The same rule applies to `scripts/n8n_api.py import FILE` and `tools/scrub_n8n_export.py` (an n8n export kept in another folder, for example Downloads, is read after you copy it into the repository folder or list its folder in `LEAD_DESK_PATH_ROOTS`).
